propsSchema.description:
Interactive map: a Mapbox basemap with every data layer (mvt, raster, geojson, h3, heatmap, arc, scatterplot, deck-geojson, fused-hex) rendered through deck.gl. Layers load data from a UDF directly (`udf`), from a tile endpoint (`tileUrl`) or from a Fused server's hex query endpoints (`fused-hex` with `source`). A DuckDB query (`sql`) reads UDF results on its own, or queries the rows of a `udf` or `fused-hex` layer (once over the tiles in view when the layer is tiled). A sidebar lets viewers restyle, filter and re-query layers at runtime.

## Example — UDF result (GeoDataFrame)

```json
{
  "type": "fused-map",
  "props": {
    "layers": [{
      "id": "buildings",
      "type": "geojson",
      "udf": "{{my_udf}}",
      "tooltip": true,
      "style": { "fillColor": { "type": "continuous", "attr": "height", "palette": "Sunset" } }
    }]
  }
}
```

## Example — UDF as H3 tiles

```json
{
  "type": "fused-map",
  "props": {
    "layers": [{
      "id": "hexes",
      "type": "h3",
      "udf": "{{my_h3_udf}}",
      "tile": true,
      "style": { "fillColor": { "type": "continuous", "attr": "count", "domain": [0, 500] } }
    }]
  }
}
```

## Example — MVT tile layer

```json
{
  "type": "fused-map",
  "props": {
    "layers": [{
      "id": "counties",
      "type": "mvt",
      "tileUrl": "https://tiles.fused.io/public/my_tileset/{z}/{x}/{y}",
      "style": { "fillColor": [100, 150, 200], "opacity": 0.6 }
    }]
  }
}
```

## Example — rows from a canvas SQL source (h3-query, sql-runner)

```json
{
  "type": "fused-map",
  "props": {
    "layers": [{
      "id": "calls",
      "type": "h3",
      "h3Column": "hex",
      "data": "{{nyc311}}",
      "sql": "SELECT hex, COUNT(*) AS calls FROM {{source}} GROUP BY 1",
      "style": { "fillColor": { "type": "continuous", "attr": "calls" } }
    }]
  }
}
```

`data: "{{name}}"` draws the rows a canvas SQL source published (an h3-query or sql-runner widget's `name`, or another map's layer id) straight from DuckDB: no UDF runs, and the edge from that node to this one grants access, as for a UDF. Add `sql` to query them (`{{source}}` or `{{name}}` reads the rows). Row layers only (geojson, h3, scatterplot, heatmap, arc, or type omitted to detect), never tiles.

## Example — SQL with lat/lng points and a filter

```json
{
  "type": "fused-map",
  "props": {
    "layers": [{
      "id": "sites",
      "type": "geojson",
      "sql": "SELECT name, lat, lng, score FROM {{my_udf}}",
      "filters": [{ "attr": "score", "type": "range", "min": 0.5 }],
      "style": { "fillColor": [255, 80, 0], "pointRadius": 8 }
    }]
  }
}
```

## Example — raster tile layer

```json
{
  "type": "fused-map",
  "props": {
    "layers": [{
      "id": "seismic",
      "type": "raster",
      "tileUrl": "https://udf.ai/<token>/run/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}?format=png",
      "style": { "opacity": 0.8 }
    }]
  }
}
```

## Example — indexed H3 data (fused-hex)

```json
{
  "type": "fused-map",
  "props": {
    "centerLng": -73.985, "centerLat": 40.755, "zoom": 14,
    "layers": [{
      "id": "nyc311-hex",
      "type": "fused-hex",
      "h3Column": "hex",
      "minZoom": 14,
      "source": {
        "path": "s3://fused-asset/data/nyc311/",
        "timeColumn": "created_date",
        "dateMin": "2024-07-01",
        "dateMax": "2024-07-07T23:59:59",
        "variables": ["complaint_type"]
      },
      "tooltip": ["hex", "created_date", "complaint_type"],
      "style": { "fillColor": { "type": "categorical", "attr": "complaint_type", "palette": "Bold" } }
    }]
  }
}
```

Raster layers with a `tileUrl` fetch PNG tiles from a publicly reachable XYZ endpoint; only `opacity` and `visible` apply. `udf` with `tile: true` instead runs the UDF per tile through the workbench (or the canvas share token on a shared page), so no share token is needed and edits to the UDF re-render the layer.

## Choosing a source

- `udf` without `tile`: one whole run. Rows become features (geojson, h3, heatmap, arc, scatterplot); an image result becomes one `raster` picture at the bounds the run returned or was sent. Not valid for `mvt` — that needs `tile: true` with `udf`, or a `tileUrl`.
- `udf` with `tile: true`: the UDF runs per XYZ tile; use for large or tile-shaped UDFs (`raster` → PNG, `h3` → hexagons, `geojson` → vector features). Tile layers never auto-fit.
- `sql`: a DuckDB query over `{{udf}}` results; rows become features via geometryColumn / latColumn+lngColumn / h3Column. On a tiled layer (`udf` with `tile: true`, or `fused-hex`) it runs once over the rows of every tile loaded in the current view, reading them as `{{source}}`, which takes no `?params` (a udf layer may also write its own `{{udf}}`, bare or with exactly its own `?params`), and runs again as the view moves (pan, zoom), the query changes or a `$param` changes; no tile is refetched. WHERE, computed columns, GROUP BY, aggregates, LIMIT, ORDER BY and USING SAMPLE apply to the whole view; only a single SELECT (a WITH is fine) over `{{source}}` runs (no UNION, several statements, other UDFs or joins), and its result must keep the cell column (h3, `fused-hex`) or the geometry columns (vector layers). `raster`, `mvt` and `tileUrl` layers take no `sql`.
- `source` (`fused-hex` only): a Fused server's ERA5, gridMET or `/h3/query` endpoint, queried per XYZ tile; no `udf` (a `sql` runs once over the tiles in view as `{{source}}`).
- Whole-run results larger than the viewer's "Max amount of data to auto-load" setting (default 50 MB) are not loaded until the viewer confirms, and UDFs marked for manual runs show a Run button instead of running on load. Prefer `tile: true`, a narrowing `sql`, or both for big datasets.

## Layer types

- `geojson`: polygons, lines and points from any source. `deck-geojson` is an alias of `geojson` and renders identically — prefer `geojson`.
- `h3`: hexagons from `h3Column` (auto-detected from common names like hex, h3 when omitted), styled with `coverage`, `extruded`, `elevationAttr`.
- `scatterplot`: point circles; `pointRadius` in pixels (default 5), `lineColor`/`lineWidth` for the outline.
- `heatmap`: density of points; weight comes from a continuous `fillColor.attr` (1 per point when fillColor is static), `pointRadius` is the kernel radius in pixels (default 30). Not pickable, no tooltip.
- `arc`: one arc per row. Give the endpoints with `startLatColumn`/`startLngColumn` and `endLatColumn`/`endLngColumn` (auto-detected from common names when omitted), or a `source`/`target` property holding [lng, lat], or a LineString whose first and second vertices are the ends. `fillColor` colours the source end, `lineColor` the target end, `lineWidth` the arc width.
- `mvt` / `raster`: tiled from `tileUrl`, or from `udf` with `tile: true`.
- `fused-hex`: H3 tiles queried from a Fused server; each XYZ tile's bbox is sent to `source.endpoint` (a full URL, or a path on the current server; /h3/query when only `source.path` is set): `/retrieve/era5/daily/bbox`, `/retrieve/era5/hourly/bbox` and `/retrieve/gridmet/bbox` serve fixed resolution 7 datasets and need `dateMin`/`dateMax`; `/h3/query` reads any indexed `source.path` and needs the layer's `h3Column` set to the location's indexed space column (set `source.timeColumn` too so latest/first can order rows). Each hex's rows over the window are reduced to one (`reduce`: latest, first, mean, min, max, sum). Tiles load from zoom 7 for a single day; unless `minZoom` is set the floor rises with the window (about one zoom level per 4x more time steps) so a tile stays within the server's row limit, and nothing draws below it. A tile the server still rejects as too large shows an error: zoom in or narrow the window. Row cells cover their hexagon whole unless `style.coverage` is set (default 1, where other hex layers default to 0.9). It reads `source` instead of `udf`; `sql` runs once over every tile in view as `{{source}}`, before `reduce`. It is never auto-detected, never auto-fits and takes no `bounds`. Set `source.overview` (`{}` is enough) to outline, from zoom 3 up to the floor, the H3 cells of the files the query reads (no rows are read): one request lists them, with no tiles and no values, so the outlines show where data exists, not what it holds. ERA5 retrieve endpoints and /h3/query only (for /h3/query the world extent lists the whole location); it is an error on gridMET. `overview.minZoom` (default 3) must sit below the floor for the overview to draw, and `overview.extent` limits the area it looks up and also confines the row tiles: none are requested outside it, and cells are drawn only inside it, overview and rows alike, by cell centre (default: the whole world). Overview cells are styled apart from the rows by `style.overview` (fused-hex only): `lineColor` and `fillColor` (default: #afafaf stroke, no fill) each take a single colour, `"inherit"` (the layer's own stroke/fill; when that is data-driven, no overview is drawn and a warning shows on the layer card, rows unaffected), or a data-driven `{type: "continuous"|"categorical", attr, ...}` colouring cells by `row_count_total` (rows in the whole file) or `row_count_window` (rows in the file sections the current date window reads) -- the only two overview attributes, since a files overview carries no other values. Also `lineWidth` (default 1, 0 = no stroke), `opacity` (the fill's, default 0.35) and `coverage` (default 1).

## Colours

`style.fillColor` and `style.lineColor` accept an [r, g, b] or [r, g, b, a] array, a CSS colour string, or a data-driven object:

- Continuous: `{ "type": "continuous", "attr": "<numeric property>", "domain": [min, max], "palette": "Sunset", "steps": 7, "reverse": false, "nullColor": "#b8b8b8", "scaleType": "quantize" }`. `domain` defaults to the data's min/max, `palette` to "Sunset". `scaleType` "quantize" (default) draws `steps` equal-width bands over the domain; "quantile" draws `steps` bands of roughly equal row count, with breaks from the data (`domain` is ignored). Sequential palettes (CARTOColors): Sunset, SunsetDark, Teal, TealGrn, Emrld, BluGrn, BluYl, Mint, DarkMint, Purp, PurpOr, Magenta, Burg, BurgYl, RedOr, OrYel, Peach, PinkYl, BrwnYl.
- Categorical: `{ "type": "categorical", "attr": "<property>", "colors": { "<value>": "#hex" }, "palette": "Bold", "nullColor": "rgba(128,128,128,0.5)" }`. Omit `colors` to auto-assign from the palette (default "Bold"). Qualitative palettes: Bold, Vivid, Safe, Prism.

A legend is drawn only for data-driven colours (see `legend` and `showLegend`).

`layerBlending` (`normal`, `additive`, `subtractive`) sets how all data layers blend where they overlap, as kepler.gl's Layer blending does; omit it for normal.
